Two Pointers.content:
## 1. Core Concept

Two pointers is an efficient technique where you use two indices to traverse the data structure, typically reducing O(n²) to O(n).

---

## 2. Types of Two Pointers

### Opposite Direction (Converging)
Pointers start at opposite ends and move toward each other.

```java
// Example: Two Sum II (Sorted Array)
int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
    int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
    if (sum == target) return new int[]{left, right};
    else if (sum < target) left++;
    else right--;
}
```

### Same Direction (Fast-Slow)
Both pointers start at same position, move at different speeds.

```java
// Example: Remove Duplicates
int slow = 0;
for (int fast = 1; fast < nums.length; fast++) {
    if (nums[fast] != nums[slow]) {
        slow++;
        nums[slow] = nums[fast];
    }
}
return slow + 1; // Length of unique elements
```

---

## 3. Classic Problems

| Problem | Technique | Key Insight |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Valid Palindrome | Converging | Skip non-alphanumeric |
| 3Sum | Sort + Converging | Fix one, two-pointer rest |
| Container With Most Water | Converging | Move shorter height |
| Remove Duplicates | Fast-Slow | Slow tracks insert position |

---

## 4. Interview Questions

**Q: Why does Container With Most Water work?**
A: Moving the shorter line might find a taller one. Moving the taller line can only decrease or maintain area.

**Q: Can Two Pointers work on unsorted arrays?**
A: For most problems, no. Sorting is often a prerequisite. Exception: sliding window variants.

Sliding Window.content:
## 1. When to Use

Use sliding window when you need to find:
- Subarray/substring with specific property
- Maximum/minimum of contiguous elements
- Problems with "contiguous" or "consecutive" keywords

---

## 2. Fixed Size Window

```java
// Maximum sum of k consecutive elements
int maxSum = 0, windowSum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    windowSum += arr[i];
}
maxSum = windowSum;

for (int i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {
    windowSum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];  // Slide window
    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);
}
```

---

## 3. Variable Size Window

```java
// Longest substring without repeating characters
Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
int left = 0, maxLen = 0;

for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    while (set.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
        set.remove(s.charAt(left));
        left++;
    }
    set.add(s.charAt(right));
    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
}
```

---

## 4. Template

```java
int left = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
    // 1. Expand: add arr[right] to window

    // 2. Shrink: while window is invalid
    while (/* condition */) {
        // remove arr[left] from window
        left++;
    }

    // 3. Update answer
}
```

---

## 5. Interview Questions

**Q: Fixed vs Variable window?**
A: Fixed: window size constant, just slide. Variable: expand right, shrink left based on condition.

**Q: Time complexity of variable sliding window?**
A: O(n) - each element is added and removed at most once.

Binary Search.content:
## 1. Classic Binary Search

```java
int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;  // Prevent overflow

        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
        else right = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;  // Not found
}
```

---

## 2. Finding Boundaries

### First Occurrence (Lower Bound)
```java
int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
while (left <= right) {
    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] >= target) {
        result = mid;
        right = mid - 1;  // Keep searching left
    } else {
        left = mid + 1;
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Binary Search on Answer

When the answer is in a range, and you can verify if a value works.

```java
// Example: Minimum days to make m bouquets
int left = 1, right = maxDay;
while (left < right) {
    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
    if (canMakeBouquets(mid)) {
        right = mid;  // Try smaller
    } else {
        left = mid + 1;
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Interview Questions

**Q: Why `left + (right - left) / 2` instead of `(left + right) / 2`?**
A: Prevents integer overflow when left and right are large.

**Q: When to use `left < right` vs `left <= right`?**
A: `<=` when searching for exact match. `<` when finding boundary (answer is in left at end).

Linked Lists.content:
## 1. Node Structure

```java
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Essential Techniques

### Dummy Node
```java
ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
dummy.next = head;
// ... operations ...
return dummy.next;  // New head
```

### Fast-Slow Pointers
```java
// Find middle
ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
}
// slow is at middle
```

### Reverse Linked List
```java
ListNode prev = null, curr = head;
while (curr != null) {
    ListNode next = curr.next;
    curr.next = prev;
    prev = curr;
    curr = next;
}
return prev;  // New head
```

---

## 3. Cycle Detection (Floyd's)

```java
boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
        if (slow == fast) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

---

## 4. Interview Questions

**Q: Find cycle start?**
A: After slow and fast meet, reset slow to head. Move both one step at a time. They meet at cycle start.

**Q: Reverse in groups of K?**
A: Count K nodes, reverse them, recursively handle rest, connect segments.

Trees.content:
## 1. Tree Basics

```java
class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left, right;

    TreeNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Traversals

### Inorder (Left-Root-Right)
```java
void inorder(TreeNode root) {
    if (root == null) return;
    inorder(root.left);
    process(root.val);  // BST: gives sorted order
    inorder(root.right);
}
```

### Preorder (Root-Left-Right)
```java
void preorder(TreeNode root) {
    if (root == null) return;
    process(root.val);
    preorder(root.left);
    preorder(root.right);
}
```

### Level Order (BFS)
```java
Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer(root);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int size = queue.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        TreeNode node = queue.poll();
        process(node.val);
        if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
        if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
    }
}
```

---

## 3. BST Properties

- Left subtree < Root < Right subtree
- Inorder traversal gives sorted sequence
- Search: O(log n) average, O(n) worst

---

## 4. Interview Questions

**Q: Validate BST?**
A: Pass min/max bounds. Each node must be within (min, max) range.

**Q: LCA of Binary Tree?**
A: Recursively check if p or q in left/right subtree. If both sides return non-null, current node is LCA.

Dynamic Programming Basics.content:
## 1. What is DP?

DP = Recursion + Memoization. It solves problems by:
1. Breaking into overlapping subproblems
2. Storing solutions to avoid recomputation

---

## 2. Top-Down (Memoization)

```java
int[] memo;

int fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    if (memo[n] != -1) return memo[n];
    memo[n] = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
    return memo[n];
}
```

---

## 3. Bottom-Up (Tabulation)

```java
int fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
    dp[0] = 0;
    dp[1] = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2];
    }
    return dp[n];
}
```

---

## 4. DP Framework

1. **Define state**: What does dp[i] represent?
2. **Recurrence relation**: How to compute dp[i] from previous states?
3. **Base case**: What are the initial values?
4. **Compute order**: Which order to fill the table?
5. **Answer**: Where is the final answer?

---

## 5. Classic Problems

| Problem | State | Recurrence |
|---------|-------|------------|
| Climbing Stairs | dp[i] = ways to reach step i | dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] |
| House Robber | dp[i] = max money from first i houses | dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-2] + nums[i]) |
| Coin Change | dp[i] = min coins for amount i | dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i-coin] + 1) |

---

## 6. Interview Questions

**Q: When to use DP?**
A: When problem has optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems.

**Q: DP vs Greedy?**
A: Greedy: local optimal leads to global optimal. DP: explores all possibilities to guarantee optimum.

Graphs.content:
## 1. Representation

### Adjacency List
```java
List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
}
graph.get(u).add(v);  // Add edge u -> v
```

---

## 2. BFS (Shortest Path in Unweighted)

```java
void bfs(int start) {
    boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.offer(start);
    visited[start] = true;

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int node = queue.poll();
        for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
            if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                visited[neighbor] = true;
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 3. DFS

```java
void dfs(int node, boolean[] visited) {
    visited[node] = true;
    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        if (!visited[neighbor]) {
            dfs(neighbor, visited);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Topological Sort (Kahn's)

```java
List<Integer> topoSort() {
    int[] indegree = new int[n];
    for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {
        for (int v : graph.get(u)) indegree[v]++;
    }

    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (indegree[i] == 0) queue.offer(i);
    }

    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int node = queue.poll();
        result.add(node);
        for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
            if (--indegree[neighbor] == 0) {
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

---

## 5. Interview Questions

**Q: Detect cycle in directed graph?**
A: DFS with colors: WHITE (unvisited), GRAY (in current path), BLACK (finished). Cycle if we visit GRAY node.

**Q: When to use BFS vs DFS?**
A: BFS: shortest path, level order. DFS: path finding, cycle detection, topological sort.

Heap.content:
## 1. Java PriorityQueue

```java
// Min Heap (default)
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

// Max Heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

// Custom comparator
PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);
```

---

## 2. Operations

```java
pq.offer(element);   // Add: O(log n)
pq.poll();           // Remove min/max: O(log n)
pq.peek();           // View min/max: O(1)
pq.size();
pq.isEmpty();
```

---

## 3. Top K Pattern

### Top K Largest (Use Min Heap of size K)
```java
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
for (int num : nums) {
    minHeap.offer(num);
    if (minHeap.size() > k) {
        minHeap.poll();  // Remove smallest
    }
}
// minHeap contains k largest elements
```

### Top K Frequent
```java
// 1. Count frequencies
Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
for (int num : nums) {
    freq.put(num, freq.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
}

// 2. Use min heap of size k
PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
    (a, b) -> freq.get(a) - freq.get(b)
);

for (int num : freq.keySet()) {
    heap.offer(num);
    if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
}
```

---

## 4. Interview Questions

**Q: Heap vs BST?**
A: Heap: O(1) find min/max, O(log n) insert/delete. BST: O(log n) for all, but supports range queries.

**Q: Find median from data stream?**
A: Use two heaps: max heap for lower half, min heap for upper half. Median is from top(s).

Backtracking.content:
## 1. Core Template

```java
void backtrack(state, choices) {
    if (isGoal(state)) {
        result.add(copy of state);
        return;
    }

    for (choice : choices) {
        if (isValid(choice)) {
            make(choice);           // Choose
            backtrack(state, remaining choices);  // Explore
            undo(choice);           // Unchoose
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Subsets

```java
void subsets(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> current) {
    result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));

    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        subsets(nums, i + 1, current);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Permutations

```java
void permute(int[] nums, boolean[] used, List<Integer> current) {
    if (current.size() == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (used[i]) continue;
        used[i] = true;
        current.add(nums[i]);
        permute(nums, used, current);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        used[i] = false;
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Combination Sum

```java
void combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target, int start, List<Integer> current) {
    if (target == 0) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }
    if (target < 0) return;

    for (int i = start; i < candidates.length; i++) {
        current.add(candidates[i]);
        combinationSum(candidates, target - candidates[i], i, current);  // Can reuse
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Interview Questions

**Q: Time complexity of generating all subsets?**
A: O(2^n) subsets, each takes O(n) to copy. Total: O(n * 2^n).

**Q: How to avoid duplicates in subsets?**
A: Sort array. Skip if `nums[i] == nums[i-1]` and `i > start`.
